<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Jing of Han</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/188-bce">188</a>–<a href="/years/141-bce">141 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Liu Qi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Jing of Han, born Liu Qi, was the sixth emperor of the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>, ruling from 157 to 141 BCE. Early in his reign he suppressed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rebellion-of-the-seven-states">Rebellion of the Seven States</a> of 154 BCE and then cut back the powers of the semi-autonomous kingdoms. His reign and his father Emperor Wen's are remembered as a period of light taxation and recovery, and he was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-jing-of-han.jpg" alt="Emperor Jing of Han" width="320" height="488" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wang Qi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SancaiJingdiHan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
